---
title: Before you begin
description: Getting a running server and your first administrator account.
section: Getting started
---

This guide assumes GateKeeper is running and you can sign in. This page covers the one-time setup
to get there. If someone has already given you a dashboard login, skip to
[Your first tenant](/docs/first-tenant).

## What has to be running

Two processes and a database:

| Piece | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| **Server** | The Rust identity provider. Serves the OAuth/OIDC endpoints and the admin API. |
| **Dashboard** | This SvelteKit app. It is a pure client of the server's admin API. |
| **PostgreSQL** | Everything is persisted here. |

The dashboard never talks to the database. It calls the server over HTTP at the URL in
`PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL`, which defaults to `http://localhost:8080`.

## Creating the first administrator

There is deliberately **no public admin signup endpoint** — if there were, anyone who could reach
your server could mint themselves an administrator. The first admin is created from the CLI, on the
machine running the server:

```bash
cargo run --bin create_admin -- admin@example.com 'a-strong-password'
```

This finds or creates a "Default Organization" and attaches the new administrator to it. You can
pass an existing organization id as a third argument to add an admin to a specific organization
instead.

> [!TIP]
> Use this same command to add colleagues later. Each administrator belongs to an organization and
> can see every tenant that organization owns.

## Signing in

Go to `/login` on the dashboard origin and use the credentials you just created. You will land on the dashboard with no
tenants yet, and an empty state inviting you to create your first one — which is exactly where the
[next page](/docs/first-tenant) picks up.

## Two settings worth knowing about now

Both live in the server's environment, not in the dashboard, and both are easy to get wrong.

> [!CAUTION]
> `REQUIRE_ADMIN_AUTH=false` downgrades the admin API guard to *best-effort*: requests are still
> attributed in the audit log, but they are no longer blocked. It exists for local development.
> Never run a real deployment with it off.

> [!WARNING]
> If `GEOIP_CITY_DB_PATH` is not set, the risk engine silently loses two of its eight signals —
> **new country** and **impossible travel** can never fire, because there is no way to resolve an
> IP to a location. The Security pages will still work; they just won't catch those cases. See
> [How risk scoring works](/docs/risk-engine).
